<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1451–1600 (from Mehmed II to the end of the 16th century)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Sultan to Enslaved</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/devshirme"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/devshirme.jpg" alt="Devshirme"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">Devshirme</a><span class="soc-credit">Ali Amir Beg (fl. 1558) · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">Ottoman society rested on one great divide. The askeri, “the military”, took in everyone who served the sultan as soldier, official or religious scholar: they paid no taxes and lived on the empire’s revenues. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reaya">reaya</a>, “the flock” of peasants, herders and townspeople, Muslim and non-Muslim alike, paid for them. Within the ruling class the highest posts went, paradoxically, to men who were legally the sultan’s slaves (kul): Christian boys taken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a>, raised as Muslims and trained in the palace, so that no hereditary nobility could rival the dynasty. Religion drew a second line. Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were protected subjects who kept their faith but paid a poll tax and ranked below Muslims in law, and the enslaved were captives from beyond the frontiers. The empire grew from about 12 million people in the 1520s to perhaps 30 million by 1600, and a few thousand men governed it.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1451–1600 (from Mehmed II to the end of the 16th century)</span><span><b>People</b> 11.7–30 million</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 9</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Padişah</span> <span class="soc-en">(The sultan)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Vezir</span> <span class="soc-en">(Viziers, governors and palace officers)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Tımarlı sipahi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Provincial cavalry)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.7–1.7%</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Ulema</span> <span class="soc-en">(Religious scholars and judges)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.3–1% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Yeniçeri</span> <span class="soc-en">(Janissaries and household troops)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">0.05–0.3%</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:8%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Esnaf</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants and guild craftsmen)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 6–10% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:83%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Reaya</span> <span class="soc-en">(Peasants and herders)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 83% (the rest, est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Zimmi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Christian and Jewish subjects)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 3–6% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-enslaved soc-est" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Köle</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
