<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mehmed-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Padişah</span> <span class="soc-en">(The sultan)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">sultan</p><p class="soc-kid">The Ottoman sultan ruled from Topkapı Palace in Istanbul and could appoint or dismiss any official.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The sultan, the padişah or “lord of kings”, was the source of every office and, in law, the owner of the empire’s farmland. He governed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> and through kanun, the dynastic law issued in his own name, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> was remembered as Kanuni, “the Lawgiver”. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in 1516–1517, the sultans also claimed the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. Everyone in the ruling class held his post, his fortune and his life at the sultan’s pleasure: Suleiman had his grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and boyhood friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pargali-ibrahim-pasha">Pargalı Ibrahim Pasha</a> strangled in 1536. From the 15th century the sultans had their heirs by slave concubines rather than by foreign princesses. No rule fixed the succession. Princes governed provinces until their father died and then fought for the throne, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> the law allowed the victor to put his brothers to death: Mehmed III had nineteen executed in 1595. From the late 16th century the sultans left day-to-day government to the grand vizier.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Only a son of the dynasty could reign, and the throne went to the prince who prevailed over his brothers, sometimes before his father’s death: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> forced his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> to abdicate in 1512.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, with the imperial family.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanun">Kanun</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/pargali-ibrahim-pasha" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pargali-ibrahim-pasha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Vezir</span> <span class="soc-en">(Viziers, governors and palace officers)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">paşa, beylerbeyi, sancakbeyi, kul</p><p class="soc-kid">Many Ottoman viziers and governors had been taken from Christian families as boys and trained in the sultan’s palace.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">At the top of the ruling class stood the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, the sultan’s deputy, with the other viziers of the imperial council (divan), the governors-general of the provinces (beylerbeyi), eight in 1527 and 32 by 1609, and the district governors (sancakbeyi) under them. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> had his grand vizier Çandarlı Halil Pasha executed in 1453, these posts went more and more to the sultan’s kul, his “slaves”: boys taken from Christian families by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and trained in the palace school, some 300 of them inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>. Here lay the paradox. The most powerful men of the empire were legally the sultan’s slaves: they commanded armies, drew revenue grants (has) worth over 100,000 akçes a year and owned slaves themselves, yet their posts, fortunes and lives were his to take. The historian Halil İnalcık argued that a convert was no longer a slave in the ordinary sense. Sokollu Mehmed Pasha, born an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, was grand vizier from 1565 to 1579.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Pages left the palace for posts as officers and governors, and a governor could rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. A word from the sultan could end a career: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> had the grand vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedik-ahmed-pasha">Gedik Ahmed Pasha</a> executed in 1482.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> A few thousand men: about 300 pages in the palace school and 1,000–2,000 in its outer colleges (Miller’s estimate), the viziers, and the governors of 8 provinces in 1527 and 32 in 1609 with their district governors; with their families roughly 0.01–0.05 per cent of 12–30 million people.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pargali-ibrahim-pasha">Pargalı Ibrahim Pasha</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koca-sinan-pasha">Koca Sinan Pasha</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadim-suleiman-pasha">Hadım Suleiman Pasha</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">Vizier</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">Devshirme</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sipahi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>0.7–1.7%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Tımarlı sipahi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Provincial cavalry)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Ottoman cavalrymen called sipahis were paid with the taxes of villages instead of wages.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The provincial cavalry, the timariot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahis</a>, made up the bulk of the classical Ottoman army. Each held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timar</a>: not land but the right to collect taxes worth up to 20,000 akçes a year from named villages, in place of a salary, in return for riding to war with armed retainers (cebelü) in proportion to his revenue; officers held larger grants (zeamet) of up to 100,000 akçes. The timariot kept order in his villages and saw that their fields stayed in cultivation, and the peasants owed some days’ work on his own plot, but he could punish no one without a judge’s verdict. A timar was not hereditary, though it was often given to a son who served, and a sipahi who missed campaigns for seven years lost it. Some timariots were Christians in the 15th century; by the 16th almost all were Muslims. Firearms and long wars reduced their value, and after the Battle of Keresztes in 1596 some 30,000 absentees were stripped of their timars.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">Timars</a> went to cavalrymen’s sons who served, and as rewards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and other servants of the sultan; a commoner who distinguished himself in war might also win one.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Some 39,000 timariots in 1475 (22,000 in Rumelia and 17,000 in Anatolia, by a contemporary claim) and about 40,000 on David Nicolle’s estimate; with households of five, roughly 0.7–1.7 per cent of 12–30 million people.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">Timar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">Sipahi</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ottoman-empire-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 0.3–1% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Ulema</span> <span class="soc-en">(Religious scholars and judges)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">kadı, müftü, müderris</p><p class="soc-kid">Ottoman judges called kadis were trained in Islamic law and settled disputes in every district of the empire.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, scholars of Islamic law trained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, formed the learned branch of the ruling class and, like the rest of it, paid no taxes. Unlike the kul, they were freeborn Muslims. They taught in the madrasas, led prayers and preached, and above all served as judges: in every district a qadi (kadı) heard cases, drew up inheritances and endowment deeds and checked the officials, for even a timariot needed his verdict to punish a peasant. Two chief judges, the kadıaskers of Rumelia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, sat in the imperial council and proposed candidates for judgeships, and the chief mufti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, the şeyhülislam, headed the hierarchy. Over the 16th century the learned career became a graded ladder from madrasa teacher to judge of a great city. Ebussuud Efendi, şeyhülislam from 1545 to 1574, worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> to reconcile the sultan’s law (kanun) with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>, and issued thousands of legal opinions (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a>), on matters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> to the drinking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Any Muslim boy could enter a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, many of them paid for by pious endowments, so the poor could rise by learning; graduates who took a town judgeship at once were paid sooner but rarely rose as high as those who began as teachers.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the judges, teachers, preachers and prayer leaders of the towns and larger villages, with their families.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">Qadi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A kadıasker, one of the two chief judges of the empire, from the Italian edition of Nicolas de Nicolay's book of Ottoman dress, 1580 · Nicolas de Nicolay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cadilesquer_a_piedi_-_Nicolay_Nicolas_De_-_1580.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-5"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/janissaries.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>0.05–0.3%</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Yeniçeri</span> <span class="soc-en">(Janissaries and household troops)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">kapıkulu</p><p class="soc-kid">Janissaries were the sultan’s elite foot soldiers, who lived in barracks and marched to the music of their own band.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The sultan’s standing army, the kapıkulu or “slaves of the Porte”, were salaried professionals: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> infantry and six divisions of household cavalry. Most came through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a>. Every few years officials took one boy from every forty Christian households, mainly in the Balkan countryside, converted him, sent him to a Turkish family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to learn the language and then trained him as a cadet. A full Janissary lived in barracks, drew his pay in cash, could not marry until Selim II allowed it in 1566, and in peacetime policed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and fought its fires; only his own officers could punish him. Legally the sultan’s slaves, the Janissaries were in practice a privileged corps: every new sultan paid them an accession gift, and they could mutiny for more, as they first did in 1449. As firearms grew in importance, so did they. The corps grew from about 6,000 men in 1475 to some 40,000 by 1609, and from 1594 freeborn Muslims and Janissaries’ own sons could join.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> rose by seniority within his unit; the best were promoted to the household cavalry or given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timars</a>, and a few rose far, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a>, levied in 1512, who commanded Janissary units before becoming the sultans’ chief architect.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> About 6,000 Janissaries in 1475 (David Nicolle) and about 40,000 by 1609, when the sultan’s standing troops of every kind numbered some 100,000; roughly 0.05–0.3 per cent of 12–30 million people, few of them married before the late 16th century.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">Devshirme</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ottoman-empire-6.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 6–10% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Esnaf</span> <span class="soc-en">(Merchants and guild craftsmen)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">tüccar</p><p class="soc-kid">Ottoman craftsmen belonged to guilds that set standards for their trades and helped members in hard times.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The townspeople were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reaya">reaya</a>, taxpayers, but the sultans needed them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> resettled Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to revive it after 1453, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> welcomed Jews expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1492, who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> and other towns. Craftsmen and shopkeepers belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> (esnaf), which kept up standards, limited production and helped members in hard times, and through which the state watched over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> and the townspeople; by the 1580s the guilds were a fixture of town life, as the account of the great procession of 1582 for the circumcision of Murad III’s son shows. Merchants traded along the overland routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and lodged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> prospered on the new trade in Yemeni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>. Jews, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were prominent in trade and finance; the Jewish financier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-nasi">Joseph Nasi</a> rose so high at court that Selim II made him duke of Naxos in 1566. The levy of boys spared the great cities and the craftsmen’s sons.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a> rights passed within families, and guilds went to court against outsiders who took up their trade; a merchant’s wealth brought comfort and influence, but not a place in the ruling class.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the merchants, craftsmen and shopkeepers of the towns, Muslim, Christian and Jewish, a minority beside the peasants.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-nasi">Joseph Nasi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">Caravanserai</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The cloth-weavers' guild parades before the sultan, Surname-i Hümayun, 1582 · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surname_338b-339a.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ottoman-empire-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 83% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Reaya</span> <span class="soc-en">(Peasants and herders)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">yörük</p><p class="soc-kid">Most people in the Ottoman Empire were farmers, who gave part of every harvest as tax.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The great majority of the sultan’s subjects were peasants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reaya">reaya</a> (“flock”) of the countryside, and they paid for the empire. All farmland belonged in law to the ruler, but a peasant family held its plot as hereditary tenants, passing it from father to son. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> a household typically gave a tithe of 10–25 per cent of its harvest to the timariot, the cavalryman who held its village, or to the treasury; it also paid a farm tax (the çift resmi for Muslims, the ispence for Christians, who owed the poll tax as well) and other dues, and owed some days’ work on the timariot’s own fields. A headman spoke for the village to the authorities, and the government tried to keep the peasants unarmed. Many Muslims were herders: in the 1570s about one household in six in four Anatolian provinces was nomadic. The late 16th century brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, crowding on the land and famine, and when demobilised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> turned to banditry, the Celali revolts spread anarchy across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from the 1590s.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The Ottoman saying ran “the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reaya">reaya</a> is a reaya”, but a Muslim villager could rise by study in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, by distinction in war or, from the late 16th century, by enlisting as a soldier.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone not counted in the other tiers: chiefly the peasants and herders, Muslim and Christian alike. Several of the other ranks are estimates, so this share is one too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reaya">Reaya</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A Greek villager with his bagpipe, from Nicolas de Nicolay's book of Ottoman dress, 1568 · after Nicolas de Nicolay · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Griekse_dorpsbewoner_met_doedelzak%2C_RP-P-1975-74-8.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ottoman-empire-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 3–6% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Zimmi</span> <span class="soc-en">(Christian and Jewish subjects)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Christians and Jews in the Ottoman Empire could follow their own religion but paid an extra tax.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a> (zimmi), protected peoples under Islamic law. They kept their churches, synagogues and property and worshipped freely, but paid a poll tax, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> (cizye), and the ispence in place of the Muslims’ farm tax; in law they could not bear arms or ride horses and had to dress apart from Muslims, though such rules were not always enforced, and the law ranked them below Muslims. They also ran much of their own affairs: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> its autonomy and lands in return for loyalty, and church and rabbinical courts judged family and communal matters, though the formal “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">millet system</a>” took shape only in the 18th and 19th centuries. Refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after 1492 made the empire’s Jews, some 150,000 by 1600, the largest Jewish community in the world. Rural Christian families also gave up sons to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> every few years. Some converted to escape these burdens; in 1565 Albanian villagers killed the levy’s officials.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> lightened taxes and opened the ruling class, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> carried some village boys to the highest offices; Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> could also prosper in trade, finance and medicine, and Jewish physicians served the sultans.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the Christians and Jews of the towns (Christian villagers are counted with the peasants); Christians were the majority in most of the Balkans and a minority in Anatolia, and there were some 150,000 Jews by 1600.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">Dhimmi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">Jizya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">Millet system</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">Devshirme</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A Jewish merchant, from Nicolas de Nicolay's book of Ottoman dress, 1568 · after Nicolas de Nicolay · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joodse_handelaar%2C_RP-P-1975-74-4.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-enslaved" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/ottoman-empire-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Enslaved</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Köle</span> <span class="soc-en">(Enslaved people)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">cariye, esir</p><p class="soc-kid">Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; many were later set free.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Islamic law forbade enslaving Muslims or the empire’s own Christian and Jewish subjects, so the enslaved were outsiders: prisoners of war, people seized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs, Africans brought across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, and the victims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>’ raids into Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which on one tentative estimate carried some 2 million people into the empire between 1500 and 1700. Sold in the slave markets, they made up perhaps a fifth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>’s people in the 16th and 17th centuries. They served as household servants and concubines, rowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and worked on farms; the ruling class were the biggest buyers, and African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> guarded the palace harem. A few rose very high: most sultans’ mothers had been slaves. A child whom a master acknowledged as his own was born free, and its mother was freed at his death. Freeing a slave was a pious act, and many were freed after a fixed term of service. Some fled, but a runaway was hard to hide.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Freedom came by manumission, often after a fixed term of service or by the owner’s will, or for a concubine whose child the owner acknowledged; freed slaves often stayed with their former owners, for few other livelihoods were open to them.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: perhaps a fifth of Istanbul’s people in the 16th and 17th centuries were slaves, but far fewer in the countryside.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">Eunuch</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">Galley</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The slave market of Constantinople, engraving by Jan Luyken, 1684 · Jan Luyken · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slave_Markt_.tif" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="ottoman-empire">Be born in the Ottoman Empire</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Ottoman society was steep but, at the top, unusually open, because the sultans recruited their rulers from outside any hereditary elite. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> could turn a Christian village boy into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>: Sokollu Mehmed Pasha, born to a Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, governed the empire as grand vizier for fourteen years, persuaded the sultan to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbian-patriarchate-of-pec">Serbian Patriarchate of Peć</a> and placed relatives, Muslim and Christian, in high posts. A captive could rise as far. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pargali-ibrahim-pasha">Pargalı Ibrahim Pasha</a>, a boy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parga">Parga</a> who came to the palace as a slave, became Suleiman’s grand vizier, and the enslaved Hürrem became the sultan’s wife. Freeborn Muslims climbed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> to the judgeships, and a commoner who distinguished himself in war might receive a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timar</a>. Conversion opened every door, and the levy of boys was hated as a “blood tax” even as some families sought it. Falls were as steep: viziers were executed and their fortunes seized, and in 1596 some 30,000 cavalrymen who had stayed away from the war lost their timars.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>Women’s lives followed their household’s rank and religion, but Islamic law gave Muslim women a legal personality of their own. They owned and inherited property, a daughter receiving half a son’s share, went to court in person and founded pious endowments: of the Ottoman fountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> still standing in the 1930s, nearly 30 per cent were women’s endowments. A peasant who left only a widow and daughters could pass on his land only if they paid an entry fee (tapu). The harem became a centre of power. Hürrem, a captive from Ruthenia, was freed and married by Suleiman around 1534, wrote to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and founded a mosque complex in Istanbul with a women’s hospital, opening the age historians call the Sultanate of Women. Jewish women such as Esther Handali were the harem’s business agents (kira). Enslaved women were at their owners’ disposal, though one who bore an acknowledged child was freed at his death.</p></section>
