<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>İnalcık, H. (1973) The Ottoman Empire: The Classical Age, 1300–1600. Trans. N. Itzkowitz and C. Imber. London: Weidenfeld and Nicolson.</li><li>İnalcık, H. and Quataert, D. (eds) (1994) An Economic and Social History of the Ottoman Empire, 1300–1914. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Imber, C. (2002) The Ottoman Empire, 1300–1650: The Structure of Power. Basingstoke: Palgrave Macmillan.</li><li>Kunt, I. M. (1983) The Sultan's Servants: The Transformation of Ottoman Provincial Government, 1550–1650. New York: Columbia University Press.</li><li>Peirce, L. P. (1993) The Imperial Harem: Women and Sovereignty in the Ottoman Empire. New York: Oxford University Press.</li><li>Braude, B. and Lewis, B. (eds) (1982) Christians and Jews in the Ottoman Empire: The Functioning of a Plural Society. 2 vols. New York: Holmes &amp; Meier.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: 11.7 million for 1520–1535, from the households in the tax registers multiplied by five; about 30 million by 1600 on a widely cited estimate.</p></section>
